Hey Orcun, Deniz,

Here is what I have tried on denoising (measurment space regularization) + joint reconstruction approach you mentioned I should explore.

L_total = L_recon + λ_fit · L_fit 

Where:

L_recon = ||mask · (L @ s_pred - d_denoised)||² -> on the valid rays

- This is the reconstruction fidelity — how well the predicted slowness field, passed through the forward model L,matches the denoised measurements
  s_pred comes from the reconstruction INR and d_denoised comes from the denoiser INR

L_fit = ||mask · (d_denoised - d_raw)||² -> on valid rays

- This is the data fidelity constraint — how far the denoiser is allowed to deviate from the raw measurements

λ_fit is the balance between these two objectives:

- High λ (e.g., 1.0): denoiser stays close to raw data → conservative, less correction, better CNR but higher MAE
- Low λ (e.g., 0.03–0.07): denoiser has more freedom to modify measurements → more mismatch correction, lower MAE but more variance and risk of drift

The problem you discovered is that the optimal λ is sample-dependent — each sample has different SoS structure, sothe mismatch between the ray-tracing L-matrix and real wave physics varies. A sample with an inclusion near the center creates different diffraction than one near the edge, so the "right" amount of denoising freedom differs

I have tried a fixed λ and it seems to have lower MAE and I have also tried cosine decay which is giving me higher CNR at the expense of MAE.

I will try to test and tune the idea on the blob and phantom data next.

I have attached the results for fixed λ and cosine decay.

Please let me know if there is anything I need to explore in this regard.



